\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{Rd}

%\VignetteEngine{knitr::knitr}
%\VignetteIndexEntry{Modular Exponentiation}
%\VignettePackage{numTheory}
%\VignetteEncoding{UTF-8}

\title{Modular Exponentiation using mod\_exp}
\author{Siddhesh Akole}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

This vignette demonstrates the \texttt{mod\_exp} function from the numTheory package.
The function computes modular exponentiation, efficiently even for large exponents. 
This operation is fundamental in number theory and cryptography.

\section{Inputs}
The function takes three numeric inputs:

\begin{itemize}
\item \texttt{base}: the number to be exponentiated.
\item \texttt{exp}: the exponent.
\item \texttt{mod}: the modulus under which the computation is performed.
\end{itemize}

\section{Example}
Here we compute a simple modular exponentiation:

<<echo=TRUE, results='verbatim'>>=
library(numTheory)

# Basic example: (2^10) mod 7
mod_exp(base = 2, exp = 10, mod = 7)

# Edge case 1: exponent = 0 → result should be 1 (since a^0 = 1)
mod_exp(base = 5, exp = 0, mod = 13)

# Edge case 2: base = 0 → result should be 0 (since 0^b mod m = 0 for b > 0)
mod_exp(base = 0, exp = 10, mod = 7)

# Edge case 3: modulus = 1 → result always 0 (since anything mod 1 = 0)
mod_exp(base = 7, exp = 5, mod = 1)
@

\section{Constraints}
In R, exact integer numerical range is: \texttt{.Machine\$integer.max} to \texttt{- .Machine\$integer.max}.  
For doubles, the numerical range is: \texttt{.Machine\$double.xmax} to \texttt{.Machine\$double.xmin}.

\section{Complexity}
The \texttt{mod\_exp} function uses fast exponentiation, which runs in 
\(\mathcal{O}(\log b)\) time, making it efficient even for very large exponents.

\section{Summary}
The \texttt{mod\_exp} function provides a reliable and efficient way to perform modular
exponentiation, making it useful for applications in number theory, algorithms, and
cryptography.

\end{document}
